          <p>
            These are typical shelf prices at the time of writing and they move with sales and tub size; check the price per serving on the listing before you buy. The bigger saving almost always comes from buying a larger tub of a store-brand or mainstream whey and using one scoop instead of two, not from chasing a discount on a premium brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            If two scoops a day feels expensive, look at the gap again. A second scoop that replaces a protein-rich meal you would have eaten anyway saves little, while one that replaces a vending-machine snack is good value. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-does-protein-powder-cost/">how much protein powder costs</a> compares tubs by cost per 25 g of protein, and our explainer on <a href="/why-is-protein-powder-so-expensive/">why protein powder is so expensive</a> shows where the money goes.
          </p>
