        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Following the scoop count on the tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Labels often suggest one to three servings a day because the brand wants the tub used up. That suggestion knows nothing about your meals. Work out the gap between your target and your food first, then use only the scoops that fill it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting shakes but not food</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                People track their shakes carefully and guess at meals, then add more powder they did not need. A single week of logging what you eat usually shows you already get more protein than you thought, and one scoop rather than two is enough.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every scoop is 25 g of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scoop sizes and protein content vary widely between brands and between flavours of the same brand. Some plant blends and weight gainers need two scoops to reach 25 g. Read the supplement facts panel before you calculate how many you need each day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting shakes replace most meals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Three or four shakes a day can hit a protein number while cutting fiber, vitamins and minerals. Keep powder to roughly a third of your daily protein so whole foods still carry most of your diet, and you will feel fuller too.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a kidney or medical condition</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Healthy kidneys handle high-protein diets well in the research, but damaged kidneys may not. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, or take medication that affects your diet, check your protein target with a doctor or dietitian before you add any daily powder.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein powder should I take a day to build muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most people building muscle need one to two scoops, or 25 to 50 g of protein, on top of a protein-rich diet. The real target is total protein of about 1.6 to 2.2 g per kilogram of body weight. Work out what your meals supply and use powder only to close the gap, ideally spread across two servings.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is 2 scoops of protein powder a day too much?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No, two scoops a day is normal for active adults and well within safe intakes for healthy people. It adds about 50 g of protein and 200 to 300 calories. It becomes too much only if it pushes your total far past what you need or replaces meals that would have supplied other nutrients.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I take protein powder every day?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Daily use is how most people take it, and there is no need to cycle off. Healthy adults can use protein powder every day as long as total protein stays sensible and the product is from a reputable, ideally third-party tested brand. People with kidney disease should check with a doctor first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many protein shakes a day should I drink?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  One or two shakes a day suits most people. One shake with about 25 g of protein is enough as a top-up for a reasonable diet. Two make sense if you train hard, are cutting calories, or eat little protein at meals. Beyond three, real meals are usually the cheaper and more filling choice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much whey protein per day is safe?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For healthy adults there is no specific whey limit; the limit is total protein. Studies have found no harm to healthy kidneys at intakes up to about 3 g per kilogram of body weight a day. In practice one to three scoops of whey is ample, and people with lactose intolerance may tolerate an isolate better than a concentrate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I take protein powder on rest days?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, if your food does not reach your target on those days too. Muscle repair continues for a day or more after training, so your protein needs do not fall much on rest days. You can drop the post-workout shake and keep one scoop at breakfast or as a snack to hold your daily total steady.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do women need less protein powder per day?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Women usually need less in absolute terms only because they tend to weigh less; the grams per kilogram are the same. A 60 kg woman who trains might aim for 85 to 120 g a day, so one scoop, sometimes two, is typical. Pregnant or breastfeeding women should confirm their target and the product with a doctor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

